{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem in a strict manner. Skewness relates to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, particularly whether more probability mass is on favorable or unfavorable outcomes relative to the overall shape. To assess skewness, we need to evaluate the direction of tail weight in each lottery. In Lottery A, there is a 0.1% chance of a large loss (-5000 Euros) and a 99.9% chance of no loss (0 Euros). This means most of the probability is on the 'better' outcome (0 Euros), but there is a small, extreme downside tail — indicating a left skew (also known as negative skew), because the left tail (unfavorable outcome) is longer, even if less probable. In Lottery B, the outcome is certain: -5 Euros with 100% probability. A degenerate (single-outcome) lottery has no skewness — it is symmetric. Thus, relevant information can be derived: Lottery A has negative (left) skew, and Lottery B has zero skew.",
  "step_2": "The decision reason prefers the lottery that is more left-skewed. A more left-skewed distribution means a longer left tail (i.e., rare but severe unfavorable outcomes) with most probability on favorable outcomes. Lottery A exhibits left skew (due to 99.9% probability on 0, which is better than the expected value, and a thin tail to the left at -5000). Lottery B is deterministic and thus has no skew. Since left skew is present in A and absent in B, and the reason prefers more left-skewed lotteries, the reason prefers Lottery A.",
  "final_answer": "A"
